# Routh II.Arts.176-179 — the principal axes referred to the invariable line
# — dynamic notebook
#
# E. J. Routh, "The Advanced Part of a Treatise on the Dynamics of a System of
# Rigid Bodies", Part II (Macmillan, 6th ed. 1905), Art. 176: "To find the
# angular motions in space of the principal axes. Since the invariable line OZ
# is fixed in space it will be convenient to refer the motion to this straight
# line as axis of Z."
#
# THE DIRECTION COSINES. Art. 177 records, from Art. 140, the angles a, b, g
# that the three principal axes make with the invariable line:
#
#     cos a = A*w1 / G       cos b = B*w2 / G       cos g = C*w3 / G
#
# These are just the body-frame components of the unit vector along L, since
# L = I w has body components (A w1, B w2, C w3). But writing them this way
# makes the point of the whole chapter visible: the invariable line is FIXED
# IN SPACE and MOVING IN THE BODY, and these three cosines are how it moves.
# They are functions of the w's, and the w's are exactly what Euler's equations
# make vary.
#
# THE TWO THINGS PRINTED SIDE BY SIDE. For the same body at the same instants:
#
#     normalize(L) in the WORLD frame  -- the invariable line, which never moves
#     normalize(L) in the BODY frame   -- the same line, sweeping the invariable
#                                         cone of Art. 159 through the body
#
# The first is constant to the last digit. The second is not, and its three
# components ARE Routh's cos a, cos b, cos g. Their squares sum to one at every
# instant, which is the only constraint they obey.
#
# Art. 178 adds the reason the motion is bounded: "Since the left-hand side of
# equation (5) is necessarily real, we see that the values of cos^2 a are
# restricted to lie between certain limits" -- the invariable line cannot point
# just anywhere in the body, only within the band the two quadrics allow.
#
# THE NUMBERS. A cuboid of mass 3 with half-extents (0.5, 1, 2) gives
# I = diag(5, 4.25, 1.25). Started tumbling at w = (0.3, 3, 0.1):
#
#     L = (1.5, 12.75, 0.125)        G = 12.838540610209558
#     invariable line in space = (0.11683570941132974,
#                                 0.9931035299963029,
#                                 0.009736309117610812)
#
# and in the body its components start at the same values -- the body begins
# unrotated -- and then move, while the space direction does not.
#
# SETUP NOTE. system.g_constant = 0 so nothing else acts. The disturbance is
# asymmetric so the motion sits off the separatrix of Art. 184 and the
# invariable line genuinely sweeps rather than creeping.
#
# WHAT YOU WATCH: a tumbling box. The line that is nailed to space is, from the
# box's own point of view, swinging around inside it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LAUNCH (run from the repository root: /Users/nsh/Developer/code/rust/rustSimulate)
#
#     posim_notebook routh_p2_principal_axes_in_space
#
# or, without the shell alias, the always-available form:
#
#     cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook dynamic_notebooks/routh_p2_principal_axes_in_space.posim
#
# The scene window opens in your browser. To start the simulation, press Start
# in that window, or type this at the In[] prompt in the terminal:
#
#     scene start
#
# If no browser should be launched (the URL is printed instead), use:
#
#     POSIM_NO_BROWSER=1 cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook dynamic_notebooks/routh_p2_principal_axes_in_space.posim
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

set system.g_constant = 0
set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15

new cuboid { mass = 3, half_extents = [0.5, 1, 2], angular_velocity = [0.3, 3, 0.1] }

get obj0.inertia_tensor
norm(obj0.angular_momentum)

# the invariable line in SPACE (fixed) and in the BODY (moving).
# The body-frame components are Routh's cos a, cos b, cos g.
normalize(obj0.angular_momentum)
[obj0.orientation.w, 0 - obj0.orientation.x, 0 - obj0.orientation.y, 0 - obj0.orientation.z] * [0, obj0.angular_momentum.x, obj0.angular_momentum.y, obj0.angular_momentum.z] * obj0.orientation

run 2 steps 2
normalize(obj0.angular_momentum)
[obj0.orientation.w, 0 - obj0.orientation.x, 0 - obj0.orientation.y, 0 - obj0.orientation.z] * [0, obj0.angular_momentum.x, obj0.angular_momentum.y, obj0.angular_momentum.z] * obj0.orientation

run 2 steps 2
normalize(obj0.angular_momentum)
[obj0.orientation.w, 0 - obj0.orientation.x, 0 - obj0.orientation.y, 0 - obj0.orientation.z] * [0, obj0.angular_momentum.x, obj0.angular_momentum.y, obj0.angular_momentum.z] * obj0.orientation

run 3 steps 3
normalize(obj0.angular_momentum)
[obj0.orientation.w, 0 - obj0.orientation.x, 0 - obj0.orientation.y, 0 - obj0.orientation.z] * [0, obj0.angular_momentum.x, obj0.angular_momentum.y, obj0.angular_momentum.z] * obj0.orientation
reset

set system.g_constant = 0
set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15
new cuboid { mass = 3, half_extents = [0.5, 1, 2], angular_velocity = [0.3, 3, 0.1] }

scene create
scene set_time_step 0.03
